Run both firmware builds with:

    pio run

Run host regression tests after the framework is installed:

    python3 test/run_native.py

The runner needs a C++17 compiler with AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Set CXX to select a compiler and ARDUINO_PICO_DIR
to use a framework outside ~/.platformio/packages/framework-arduinopico.
When running under ptrace, disable unsupported leak detection with:

    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 python3 test/run_native.py

Tests use the production protocol, config, and Bluetooth transport sources.
Hardware-facing APIs are replaced with fakes; transport tests use the installed
BTstack headers to check signatures and decode real event layouts. The fake
radio checks lock ownership, retains L2CAP SDU buffers across sends, and can
invoke notification/can-send callbacks synchronously or later.

Coverage includes sequence rollover, duplicate/stale rejection, all 512 fixed
control-field combinations and their truncated forms, motor/servo limits,
all 181 servo angles through the protocol and actual XRPServo conversion,
sensor serialization, timing echoes, failed renames, INI quoting and integer
overflow, atomic config replacement failures, advertising limits, MTU limits,
CCCD validation, foreign writes, prepared/offset writes, pending channel
reservation, buffer lifetime, coalescing, callback ordering, receive overflow,
and disconnect/reconnect cleanup.

Hardware verification still needed before release:
- Connect with GATT and L2CAP clients, stream control/status traffic, and verify
  sensor values, watchdog output shutoff, and reconnect behavior.
- Exercise small and sufficient ATT MTUs, subscribe/unsubscribe, an additional
  connection attempt, and L2CAP credit starvation during status streaming.
- Rename while controls are streaming; outputs must remain off until reboot.
  Verify the advertised name and persisted config after reboot.
- Verify USB serial diagnostics and the idle status-file snapshot on both boards.
